WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.426
Every payment has to write a row to the ledger database.

2
00:00:06.426 --> 00:00:10.884
It has room for 20 connections, so it can serve 20 calls at a time.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.337
You have a batch of 5,000 payments.

4
00:00:13.337 --> 00:00:16.852
With virtual threads, each payment gets its own thread.

5
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:18.374
That part is cheap.

6
00:00:18.374 --> 00:00:21.816
Each virtual thread is a small object on the heap.

7
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:26.648
So how many of them ask the database for a connection at the same moment?

8
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:29.452
All 5,000.

9
00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:32.435
They all go straight for the database.

10
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:35.789
That is far more than the 20 it has room for.

11
00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:37.315
The threads are cheap.

12
00:00:37.315 --> 00:00:38.703
The database is not.

13
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:42.763
Think of the front desk of an office building.

14
00:00:42.763 --> 00:00:45.963
It keeps 20 visitor badges, one for each connection.

15
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.741
To go in, a thread must take a badge.

16
00:00:48.741 --> 00:00:51.385
The first 20 each take one, and go in.

17
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:53.099
Now the desk is empty.

18
00:00:53.099 --> 00:00:55.231
What happens to the next thread?

19
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:57.664
It waits.

20
00:00:57.664 --> 00:01:01.025
On a virtual thread, that wait parks the thread.

21
00:01:01.025 --> 00:01:03.476
A parked thread holds no OS thread.

22
00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:08.239
It stays a small object on the heap, so 5,000 waiters are cheap too.

23
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:12.479
When a call finishes, the thread hands its badge back on the way out.

24
00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:15.328
That wakes one waiter, and it takes the badge in.

25
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:18.721
Calls finish, badges come back, waiters go in.

26
00:01:18.721 --> 00:01:21.285
The database never sees more than 20.

27
00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:25.928
This desk is a Semaphore.

28
00:01:25.928 --> 00:01:28.041
Its badges are called permits.

29
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:31.359
acquire takes a permit, and waits if none is left.

30
00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:34.019
release gives one back, and wakes a waiter.

31
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:40.053
In code, the semaphore is one field on the client, shared by every request.

32
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.071
acquire comes before the try.

33
00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:47.981
If a thread is interrupted while it waits, it never got a permit, so it must not give one back.

34
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.625
release goes in finally, so the permit always comes back, even when the call throws.

35
00:01:53.833 --> 00:02:01.264
And tryAcquire with a timeout returns false when no permit came in time, so you can answer with a fast 503.

36
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:02.768
The rule.

37
00:02:02.768 --> 00:02:08.580
One virtual thread per task, and one shared semaphore in front of each scarce resource.

38
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:09.805
Check yourself.

39
00:02:09.805 --> 00:02:14.293
A handler creates a new semaphore with 20 permits for each request.

40
00:02:14.293 --> 00:02:17.375
How many calls can reach the database at once?

41
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.119
As many as there are requests.

42
00:02:22.119 --> 00:02:26.897
Each request gets its own desk with 20 badges, so no thread ever waits.
